# 1. First Dockerfile

## Objective

Create a custom Ubuntu-based Docker image that:

1. Uses Ubuntu as the base image
2. Installs `curl`
3. Sets the working directory to `/app`
4. Prints a custom message when the container starts

## Project Structure

```text
my-first-image/
└── Dockerfile
```

## Dockerfile

```dockerfile
FROM ubuntu

WORKDIR /app

RUN apt-get update && \
    apt-get install curl -y

CMD ["echo", "Hello from my custom image!"]
```

## Understanding the Instructions

### `FROM`

Defines the base image used to build the custom image.

```dockerfile
FROM ubuntu
```

Every Dockerfile requires a base image from which the custom image is built.

### `WORKDIR`

Sets the working directory inside the image/container.

```dockerfile
WORKDIR /app
```

Subsequent Dockerfile instructions operate relative to this directory unless another directory is specified.

### `RUN`

Executes commands **during image build time**.

```dockerfile
RUN apt-get update && \
    apt-get install curl -y
```

The result of the `RUN` instruction becomes part of the image.

### `CMD`

Defines the default command that runs when the container starts.

```dockerfile
CMD ["echo", "Hello from my custom image!"]
```

---

## Build the Image

```bash
docker build -t my-ubuntu:v1 .
```

The image was successfully built and tagged as:

```text
my-ubuntu:v1
```

This also demonstrated Docker's build process and layer caching.

## Run the Container

```bash
docker run --name ubuntu-cont my-ubuntu:v1
```

Output:

```text
Hello from my custom image!
```

### Result

This verified that the `CMD` instruction executes when the container starts.

